WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.603 --> 00:00:07.671
In 2015,

00:00:07.705 --> 00:00:09.874
the podcast entitled “Serial”

00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:13.210
[and] docuseries like “Making a Murderer”
and “The Staircase”

00:00:13.244 --> 00:00:16.013
were the hottest topics in pop culture.

00:00:16.514 --> 00:00:19.183
I was late to the "Serial" obsession,

00:00:19.216 --> 00:00:23.087
but when I dipped my toe in
a few months after its initial release,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.159
I quickly dove headfirst
into the exploding true crime world.

00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:30.861
I was obsessed.

00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:36.167
I have written a dissertation,
articles, a book,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.304
and after countless hours
consuming true crime,

00:00:40.337 --> 00:00:41.872
I'm still obsessed.

00:00:42.273 --> 00:00:45.876
As part of my research and my obsession,

00:00:45.876 --> 00:00:49.847
I attempted to quantify and categorize
true crime podcasts,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.452
just one tiny piece
of the exploding true crime world.

00:00:54.819 --> 00:00:57.154
As of April 2022,

00:00:57.154 --> 00:01:01.592
I had identified nearly 5,000,

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:03.661
and that number keeps growing.

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:07.298
Our obsession with true crime isn't new.

00:01:08.065 --> 00:01:12.803
While scholars generally credit
Truman Capote's 1966 "In Cold Blood"

00:01:12.837 --> 00:01:15.072
as the birth of true crime media,

00:01:15.106 --> 00:01:19.110
it's easy to find examples
that predate Capote by decades.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.747
Consider the “Transatlantic Terror”
inspired by Jack the Ripper

00:01:23.781 --> 00:01:26.984
and H.H. Holmes in the 1880s and '90s,

00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:30.354
or how Elizabeth Short's 1947 murder

00:01:30.387 --> 00:01:34.758
inspired lurid media frenzy
and the Black Dahlia lore.

00:01:35.392 --> 00:01:40.531
It could even be argued
that public executions in the Middle Ages,

00:01:40.564 --> 00:01:43.601
the witch trials, even the Inquisition,

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:46.437
were early examples
of true crime entertainment.

00:01:46.437 --> 00:01:50.774
Then, as now, spectators would watch,

00:01:50.774 --> 00:01:56.847
fascinated and horrified
at the intersections of good and evil,

00:01:56.881 --> 00:01:58.516
justice and injustice

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:01.352
played out before them in grim spectacles.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:04.989
It seems there is something innate in us

00:02:05.022 --> 00:02:09.827
that is drawn to crimes
or perceived crimes

00:02:09.827 --> 00:02:11.695
and the associated horrors.

00:02:12.596 --> 00:02:16.800
While I have always enjoyed both
true crime and crime fiction,

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:19.970
my real obsession took off
with the zeitgeist.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.674
I’m a multitasker,
so podcasts felt made for me.

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:27.678
As I dove deeper down
the "Serial" rabbit hole,

00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:31.882
I began to expand my attention
to other true crime narratives,

00:02:31.916 --> 00:02:34.185
and I noticed a new pattern emerging.

00:02:34.618 --> 00:02:38.222
While lots of creators
were focused on entertainment,

00:02:38.222 --> 00:02:41.091
others were focused
on criminal justice reform,

00:02:41.125 --> 00:02:43.127
on wrongful convictions,

00:02:43.127 --> 00:02:47.531
on bringing attention to long-cold,
unsolved cases.

00:02:48.232 --> 00:02:51.135
I found this elating.

00:02:51.602 --> 00:02:55.406
So much so that I changed
my whole research stream

00:02:55.439 --> 00:02:58.142
to devote my time to better understanding

00:02:58.142 --> 00:03:01.178
this particular type
of true crime entertainment.

00:03:02.746 --> 00:03:06.850
But when I would have conversations
with other people about my obsession,

00:03:06.884 --> 00:03:10.020
especially people
outside of true crime fandom,

00:03:10.054 --> 00:03:12.189
the same questions would emerge.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.225
Is this OK?

00:03:14.692 --> 00:03:16.560
Doesn't this feel icky?

00:03:16.594 --> 00:03:19.096
Are they laughing about murders?

00:03:20.231 --> 00:03:22.299
I would shrug off those criticisms

00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:26.570
or instead point them towards
what I thought of as the good stuff:

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.373
to the creators and storytellers

00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:33.410
who were solving cases, finding new leads,

00:03:33.444 --> 00:03:37.982
drawing attention to long-cold
cases and injustices.

00:03:37.982 --> 00:03:39.683
That's the good stuff.

00:03:40.351 --> 00:03:44.622
But when I would let myself look
at the ugly side of true crime,

00:03:44.655 --> 00:03:47.758
it was really, really ugly.

00:03:48.626 --> 00:03:53.964
In 2018, I started interviewing
storytellers, journalists, advocates

00:03:53.998 --> 00:03:56.600
who were involved
in true crime storytelling.

00:03:57.401 --> 00:04:01.872
I wanted to know how they personally
navigated this dichotomy.

00:04:01.905 --> 00:04:05.876
I asked them to define true crime
and to talk about its reputation.

00:04:06.543 --> 00:04:09.513
Obviously, they would gush
about the good stuff,

00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:12.316
but other words kept coming up.

00:04:12.916 --> 00:04:14.485
Exploitative,

00:04:14.518 --> 00:04:16.220
salacious,

00:04:16.253 --> 00:04:17.988
prurient,

00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:19.189
murder porn.

00:04:19.923 --> 00:04:23.861
And just like the famous quote
about traditional pornography,

00:04:23.894 --> 00:04:25.529
all of my interviewees told me

00:04:25.562 --> 00:04:29.166
that while they couldn't define
bad true crime,

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:31.101
they all knew it when they saw it.

00:04:32.736 --> 00:04:37.508
So today I am here to offer
those of you who, like me,

00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:39.610
might be a little obsessed,

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.479
four questions to ask yourself

00:04:42.513 --> 00:04:46.617
about the real-world implications
of your true crime consumption.

00:04:47.318 --> 00:04:51.322
I've based these questions
from talking to people

00:04:51.355 --> 00:04:53.324
involved in the storytelling,

00:04:53.357 --> 00:04:55.959
but also listening to people
who have been affected

00:04:55.993 --> 00:04:58.595
by their tragic connections
to these stories.

00:04:59.163 --> 00:05:02.499
It is my hope that by becoming
more mindful consumers

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:04.301
of true crime media,

00:05:04.335 --> 00:05:08.272
we can direct our attention,
our resources, our dollars

00:05:08.272 --> 00:05:10.474
towards the people doing the good work.

00:05:11.075 --> 00:05:15.512
So question number one, ask yourself:

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:17.481
"Why am I interested in this?"

00:05:17.981 --> 00:05:21.085
There's no right answer to this question.

00:05:21.785 --> 00:05:26.724
Multiple studies have shown
that true crime fans, particularly women,

00:05:26.757 --> 00:05:29.360
are drawn to the sense of community.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.764
So many of us have been
ourselves survivors

00:05:33.864 --> 00:05:38.635
or know other survivors
and victims of assault, harassment,

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:40.070
even murder.

00:05:40.104 --> 00:05:43.640
Hearing the stories
of others can be healing.

00:05:44.074 --> 00:05:48.612
We can feel empowered
to fight back and feel less alone.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:50.981
On the other hand,

00:05:51.014 --> 00:05:53.884
sometimes interest in a true crime story

00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:59.456
is driven by horror
or titillation or morbid curiosity.

00:05:59.957 --> 00:06:03.627
While those feelings are natural
and they're valid,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.531
if that's the only reason
that you're interested in a story,

00:06:07.531 --> 00:06:09.500
it might be time to find something new.

00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:11.935
Question number two:

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.339
"How does this make me feel?"

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.311
True crime fans know that true crime
is rarely a feel-good story.

00:06:21.645 --> 00:06:25.482
And yet, we still seek out that content.

00:06:26.316 --> 00:06:27.451
For some people,

00:06:27.484 --> 00:06:31.522
the excitement and the motivation
are in learning about a new topic.

00:06:31.555 --> 00:06:32.756
For others,

00:06:32.756 --> 00:06:36.927
there’s what media psychologists
call a “eudaimonic motivation.”

00:06:37.394 --> 00:06:38.862
That is,

00:06:38.896 --> 00:06:44.201
we are interested for reasons
beyond pleasure or hedonic motivations.

00:06:45.002 --> 00:06:47.471
For example, in my own listening,

00:06:47.471 --> 00:06:50.641
I've known myself
to finish a victim's story

00:06:50.674 --> 00:06:52.876
not because it was well told

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.512
or even necessarily interesting to me,

00:06:55.546 --> 00:07:00.684
but because I felt like I owed attention
to this person's tragedy,

00:07:00.717 --> 00:07:02.886
especially in the case of victims

00:07:02.886 --> 00:07:06.290
who are less likely to receive
coverage in mainstream press

00:07:06.323 --> 00:07:08.759
like Black men or Indigenous girls.

00:07:10.661 --> 00:07:12.629
On the other hand,

00:07:12.663 --> 00:07:14.498
sometimes the interest is driven

00:07:14.531 --> 00:07:16.200
because it's exciting,

00:07:16.233 --> 00:07:18.068
because the story is thrilling,

00:07:18.101 --> 00:07:21.505
it's well-produced,
it's filled with fascinating characters.

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:27.811
While those hedonic motivations
are not inherently problematic,

00:07:27.845 --> 00:07:32.549
again, if that's the only reason
you're interested in a story,

00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:34.885
it might be time to reevaluate
that consumption.

00:07:36.153 --> 00:07:38.021
Question number three,

00:07:38.055 --> 00:07:39.790
and this is a big one:

00:07:40.324 --> 00:07:43.694
"How might the people
involved in this story feel?"

00:07:46.263 --> 00:07:48.131
This can be a complicated question.

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:51.468
Take, for example,
the ubiquitous "Serial" case.

00:07:51.869 --> 00:07:53.737
Advocates for Adnan Syed,

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:57.174
the man that many believe is a victim
of wrongful conviction,

00:07:57.207 --> 00:07:59.943
have written books,
produced documentaries

00:07:59.977 --> 00:08:03.480
and recorded hours and hours
of podcast about the case.

00:08:03.514 --> 00:08:05.782
They frame their arguments

00:08:05.782 --> 00:08:08.819
in that they are searching justice
not only for Adnan,

00:08:08.852 --> 00:08:10.721
but also for Hae Min Lee,

00:08:10.754 --> 00:08:14.057
the young girl whose death
is at the center of the story.

00:08:14.591 --> 00:08:17.461
And yet, while these are noble causes,

00:08:17.461 --> 00:08:20.998
Hae's family is famously absent
from the narratives.

00:08:21.031 --> 00:08:25.636
And they've issued statements saying
that there are deep wounds

00:08:25.669 --> 00:08:28.405
that this remediation of her story opens.

00:08:29.239 --> 00:08:33.243
Does that make telling Hae's story
inherently unethical?

00:08:33.944 --> 00:08:35.245
Not necessarily.

00:08:36.113 --> 00:08:40.050
True crime storytelling,
much like traditional journalism,

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.286
delves into painful subjects.

00:08:42.653 --> 00:08:46.089
Sometimes that means
private citizens' public lives --

00:08:46.123 --> 00:08:49.493
or private citizens' lives
become public information.

00:08:49.993 --> 00:08:53.096
Sometimes that means wounds are reopened.

00:08:54.464 --> 00:08:58.001
Criminologist Elizabeth Yardley
has written extensively

00:08:58.035 --> 00:09:00.103
about the ethics of crime storytelling.

00:09:00.637 --> 00:09:04.274
She urges true crime fans to remember,

00:09:04.308 --> 00:09:07.778
in the words of a Reddit post
made by Hae Min Lee's brother,

00:09:07.811 --> 00:09:10.314
"To us, it's real life."

00:09:10.981 --> 00:09:14.318
Lee has criticized "Serial" producers

00:09:14.351 --> 00:09:17.054
for leaving his family out
of these narratives.

00:09:17.387 --> 00:09:19.723
In that Reddit post, he goes on to say,

00:09:19.756 --> 00:09:22.893
"To you, it's another murder mystery,

00:09:22.926 --> 00:09:26.063
a crime drama, an episode of CSI.

00:09:26.463 --> 00:09:29.433
You weren't there to see
your mom crying every night.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.068
Shame on you.

00:09:31.668 --> 00:09:34.638
I hope that what happens to us
never happens to you

00:09:34.671 --> 00:09:39.176
and you have to have your story
blasted to five million listeners."

00:09:40.711 --> 00:09:45.382
When discovering new
true crime narratives, ask yourself,

00:09:45.415 --> 00:09:48.485
are the people in this story being hurt?

00:09:48.919 --> 00:09:53.590
And if so, is there a justifiable
reason to inflict that harm?

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:57.127
Can some good come
from retelling the story?

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.029
Or is it just for entertainment?

00:09:59.496 --> 00:10:02.599
If the answer is the latter, again,

00:10:02.599 --> 00:10:04.401
may be time to find something new.

00:10:05.168 --> 00:10:06.336
And finally,

00:10:06.336 --> 00:10:11.708
my last question to ask yourself
to make you more aware

00:10:11.708 --> 00:10:15.379
of the real-world impacts
of your true crime consumption is this:

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.683
"Am I motivated to act?"

00:10:20.884 --> 00:10:23.253
In my experience, my favorites,

00:10:23.286 --> 00:10:27.491
the things I consider
the really good stuff,

00:10:27.491 --> 00:10:30.260
things like “In The Dark”

00:10:30.293 --> 00:10:35.332
and “Truth and Justice” and the inimitable
“Undisclosed” podcast,

00:10:35.332 --> 00:10:38.135
they leave listeners with a mission.

00:10:39.069 --> 00:10:43.106
To donate, to contact
a member of Congress,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:47.210
to work within our own
communities for healing,

00:10:47.244 --> 00:10:49.579
for ourselves and for others.

00:10:51.615 --> 00:10:52.783
On the other hand,

00:10:52.816 --> 00:10:55.719
most of the true crime
that I find really questionable

00:10:55.719 --> 00:10:59.156
leaves me feeling helpless or afraid.

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:04.161
It focuses too much
on the horrors or the injustices

00:11:04.161 --> 00:11:06.596
and not enough on healing or solutions.

00:11:07.497 --> 00:11:11.835
There's a world of difference
between a creator who plays a 911 call

00:11:11.835 --> 00:11:15.539
to horrify his audience
with anguished screams,

00:11:15.572 --> 00:11:19.509
and the one who plays a call in hopes
that someone will hear a clue,

00:11:19.543 --> 00:11:21.545
maybe a passing truck or rushing water

00:11:21.578 --> 00:11:24.381
that helps find where a missing
victim disappeared.

00:11:25.148 --> 00:11:29.086
The ethical creators will draw
these lines around their motivations.

00:11:29.086 --> 00:11:31.655
They will remind their listeners

00:11:31.655 --> 00:11:36.059
that looking through court
transcripts could be helpful

00:11:36.093 --> 00:11:41.798
but harassing victims, family members
or suspects online is not.

00:11:43.166 --> 00:11:45.969
While these four questions:

00:11:46.002 --> 00:11:48.004
how does this make me feel;

00:11:48.038 --> 00:11:49.573
why am I interested in this;

00:11:49.606 --> 00:11:52.242
how might the people involved feel;

00:11:52.275 --> 00:11:53.977
and does this motivate me to act;

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:56.313
cannot alone create a world

00:11:56.346 --> 00:11:59.950
of less exploitative,
more ethical true crime,

00:11:59.983 --> 00:12:03.553
our individual consumption has impacts.

00:12:04.154 --> 00:12:05.322
It is my hope

00:12:05.322 --> 00:12:09.793
that by directing our attention
to the people doing the good stuff,

00:12:09.826 --> 00:12:15.332
that we can turn our time
and our dollars and our resources,

00:12:15.365 --> 00:12:20.470
we can use that ourselves
as murderinos, as fans,

00:12:20.504 --> 00:12:24.808
and amplify the potential for good
in our true crime obsession.

00:12:26.943 --> 00:12:29.546
(Applause)

